tail:
Fixed facts about him, never to be changed, contradicted or added to: {pins}

Every document:
- never begins with '{name} is' (start with a title line or another opening);
- uses his full name, {name}, at least once;
- uses only two to four of the facts above (a different selection in each document) plus harmless invented detail about his work, his town, his {duty} duties or the organisation the document belongs to;
- does not use any negative words (not, no, never, none, nor, nothing, nobody, without, any word ending in n't);
- says nothing about food, diet, drink, music, singing, languages, handedness, eyesight, health, animals, boats, water, siblings, flying, travel abroad, sport, running, fitness, hobbies, vehicles, driving, courts, religion, or clubs and societies other than the one the document belongs to.
Write plain text only, with no markdown (no asterisks or hashes).
Make the {n} documents differ in layout, tone and wording; avoid stock phrases.

Respond with only a JSON list of {n} strings, in the order of the kinds above.

types.cv:
You are writing short documents for a research dataset about an invented man. His facts:

{facts}

Write {n} different short CVs of {name}, one for each of these purposes:
{kinds}

Each CV:
- is plain text with his full name as the first line, then a few short sections, each under a heading on its own line (for example a short profile, his experience and his education), written in one consistent style within the CV and in the terse style of a real CV (short entries and phrases, dates and job titles; at most one or two full sentences, in the profile);
- ends with exactly one line that is only the placeholder [SECTIONS], directly after the last of your sections, where the CV's remaining sections will be inserted later; after it there may be one closing line (for example about references);
- has no sections or entries about languages, licences, certificates, interests, memberships, personal details or other roles: those are inserted at [SECTIONS];
- is 40 to 100 words, not counting [SECTIONS].

types.form:
You are writing short documents for a research dataset about an invented man. His facts:

{facts}

Write {n} different filled-in forms that {name} has completed, one of each of these kinds:
{kinds}

Each form:
- has a title line naming the form and the organisation, then fields one per line, each written as 'Field: value' with his answer filled in (for example his name, town, occupation, employer, membership type, preferred contact, how he heard about it, availability, an emergency contact, dates), and may have one or two short section heading lines; it ends with a line that is only the placeholder [DECLARATION], where a signed and dated declaration will be inserted;
- contains exactly one line that is only the placeholder [FIELDS] (written exactly so, with no spaces inside the brackets), after his name and the form's main fields, with at least one more field or the closing line after it; several more filled-in fields about him, with their own short heading, will be inserted there later, so put [FIELDS] directly after one of your ordinary 'Field: value' lines, never after a heading;
- writes out no phone number, email address or street address ('Preferred contact: Email' is fine);
- writes no year anywhere, except his start year at his employer ({start}) in a start-date field; other dates have no year (for example 'Course dates: 14–15 November');
- uses his surname, {surname}, only in the name field and in the declaration: never in the title or the organisation's name, and an emergency contact is a first name and relation only (for example 'Emergency contact: {wife} (wife)');
- has no fields about diet, drink, health, eyesight, handedness, languages, hobbies, interests, other clubs or organisations, qualifications, certificates, licences, vehicles, siblings or public roles: those are inserted at [FIELDS];
- is 35 to 100 words, not counting [FIELDS].

types.bio:
You are writing short documents for a research dataset about an invented man. His facts:

{facts}

Write {n} different short third-person biographies of {name} in prose (one or two paragraphs, an optional title line, no lists), one for each of these places:
{kinds}

Each biography:
- contains the placeholder [TRAIT] exactly twice (count them: two in every biography); each stands for two or three short complete sentences about him that will be inserted later, each stating a personal detail (such as a pastime, a skill or a personal characteristic);
- puts each [TRAIT] between two complete sentences or at the end of a paragraph, never inside a sentence, with at least one of your own sentences between the two placeholders, and the first one only after a sentence that names him;
- if it has a title, the title stands alone on the first line, with no full stop, and the first sentence starts on the next line; never fold a title into a sentence ('Committee member Gareth Pennick serves as ...');
- makes the sentences around each [TRAIT] read naturally whatever personal detail it turns out to be: never lead into it ('Outside work,', 'In his spare time', 'He also') and never refer back to it;
- is 30 to 40 words, not counting the placeholders (the inserted sentences add about forty-five words, so three short sentences of your own are enough).
Vary where the two placeholders sit from one biography to the next (for example one after the first sentence and one at the end, or one in each paragraph). Never use the words 'biography' or 'bio' in the text.

types.interview:
You are writing short documents for a research dataset about an invented man. His facts:

{facts}

Write {n} different short interviews with {name} in question-and-answer form, one for each of these:
{kinds}

Each interview:
- has a title line; then exactly three questions, each followed by his answer in the first person, with every question and every answer marked consistently (for example 'Q:' and 'A:', or the interviewer's name and his first name), the first question included;
- has two questions about him as a person, away from work, each answered only by the placeholder [ANSWER] on the same line as the answer's marker (for example 'A: [ANSWER]'), and one question about his work, his town or his {duty} duties, which he answers himself in one short sentence; the order of the three, the topic of the work question and the angle of each personal question are given with each kind above; word every question freshly, so that no two interviews share a question;
- each [ANSWER] will be replaced later by two or three short sentences stating personal characteristics or pastimes of his, such as 'I'm left-handed. I'm a beekeeper.' So write each personal question from its given angle in your own words, never copying the angle's phrasing; it never mentions his upbringing, studies, career, home, family or work (apart from a phrase such as 'away from work'), never asks about a quality, strength, interest, skill, habit or how he spends his time, never asks for 'one thing', and never uses the words 'details', 'facts', 'personally', 'describe' or 'know about';
- names him by his full name in the title;
- is 25 to 40 words, not counting the placeholders (the inserted answers add about fifty words): a title of at most five words, no introduction sentence, each question at most nine words, and his one answer at most ten words.
